// Comparing secrets without telling the caller where they differ.
//
// An equality check that returns as soon as it finds a mismatch leaks the position of that mismatch
// in how long it took. For a MAC tag that is the whole attack: forge a tag, learn the first byte was
// right from the reply being a fraction slower, keep the byte and move on — sixteen bytes fall in a
// few thousand attempts instead of 2^128.
//
// **This is not a constant-time guarantee, and saying so is the point.** wac compiles to WasmGC and
// offers no control over what a JIT does with a loop; a compiler is free to introduce a branch we did
// not write. What this removes is the *obvious* leak — the early return — which is the one an attacker
// can actually measure over a network. The stronger property needs a language that promises it, and
// nothing here promises it.
//
// There were four copies of this loop before this file: two in `crypto` (`aead`, `aesgcm`), one in
// `tor` (`hsservice`), and a fifth was about to be written in `hsreplay` that was **not**
// constant-time, because a replay cache does not look like a place where timing matters. It is: the
// digests it compares are of ciphertext an attacker chose.
//
// The related trap that made this worth a file rather than another copy — a truncated comparison,
// checking four bytes of a thirty-two-byte digest — cannot be caught by any test that feeds real
// inputs, because constructing two values whose digests share a prefix is the work the digest exists
// to prevent. It can only be caught by a test that knows how many bytes were compared, which is what
// this module's own test does, and which every caller then inherits.

/**
 * Whether `a` and `b` are the same bytes, without an early exit.
 *
 * Different lengths return false immediately, which is deliberate rather than an oversight: a length
 * is not a secret — it is visible in the message — and looping to a length that does not exist would
 * mean deciding what to compare against, which is worse than admitting the sizes differ.
 */
export bool ctEqual(u8[] a, u8[] b) {
  if (a.len() != b.len()) { return false; }
  i32 diff = 0;
  for (i32 i = 0; i < a.len(); i++) { diff = diff | (a[i] ^ b[i]); }
  return diff == 0;
}

/**
 * The same, for a fixed expected length — for callers that know the size and want it enforced.
 *
 * `gcmDecrypt` and `aeadOpen` both compare exactly sixteen bytes and both trap on a tag of any other
 * length. Passing the length here says that intent at the call site rather than leaving a bare 16 in
 * a loop bound, where shortening it is an edit nobody would question.
 */
export bool ctEqualN(u8[] a, u8[] b, i32 n) {
  if (a.len() < n || b.len() < n || n <= 0) { return false; }
  i32 diff = 0;
  for (i32 i = 0; i < n; i++) { diff = diff | (a[i] ^ b[i]); }
  return diff == 0;
}
